# My bonny is over the ocean - Schots-Engels volksliedje
# Traditioneel. Volgens Wikipedia (en, "My Bonnie Lies over the Ocean") een traditioneel
# lied (Roud 1422) zonder bekende auteur; oudste gedocumenteerde druk 1881, bladmuziek
# "Bring Back My Bonnie to Me" van Charles E. Pratt onder de schuilnamen H. J. Fuller en
# J. T. Wood. Het lied zelf en de melodie zijn publiek domein.
# De parodiecoupletten onder "Andere versie" zijn anoniem en pas na 1900 in druk gevonden
# (Internet Archive): over tuberculose in Macdonald College Magazine (1911-1912), over
# zeeziekte in het jaarboek The Granois (1914) en A History of Base Hospital 32 (1922),
# over de gastank in The Morse Dry Dock Dial (1921-1922) en over de voeten uit het raam in
# Trigger Berg and the Cockeyed Ghost (1933). Voor de coupletten over de oma in de kelder
# en over het ontbijt is geen druk gevonden. Als anonieme varianten op een vrij lied
# gelden ze als publiek domein. Meld het als u de rechthebbende van de parodieën kent.
# Overgenomen uit MY_BON.NY (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991) en My Bonny is over the
# ocean.doc (Word 97-2003, 2005); de teksten zijn gelijk. Akkoorden bij het eerste
# couplet en het refrein, geplaatst op de kolommen van de WP-versie en verschoven
# naar de dichtstbijzijnde lettergreep; "Ami" is als "Am" geschreven. Tikfouten
# hersteld: brough, row, law, stucck, feed, thr, stil en tra werden brought, raw,
# lay, stuck, feet, the, still en tea. De spelling bonny/bonnie volgt de bron.
{title: My bonny is over the ocean}
{meta: id my-bonny-is-over-the-ocean}
{meta: language en}
{lyricist: traditioneel}
{composer: traditioneel}
{copyright: Public domain}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: source Overgenomen uit MY_BON.NY (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991) en My Bonny is over the ocean.doc (2005); herkomst via Wikipedia (en, My Bonnie Lies over the Ocean); oudste drukken van de parodieën 1911-1933 (Internet Archive)}
{meta: tag kampvuur}
{meta: tag volkslied}
{key: G}

{start_of_verse}
[G]My bonny is o[C]ver the o[G]cean
My [A7]bonny is o[D7]ver the sea
[G]My bonny [C]is [G]over the [Am]ocean, oh bring back [D7]my bonny to [G]me
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[G]Bring back, [C]bring [A7]back
[D7]oh bring back my bon[G]ny [D7]to [G]me to me
Bring [C]back, [A7]bring back
oh [D7]bring back my bonny to [G]me
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Oh, blow ye winds over the ocean
Oh, blow ye winds over the sea
Oh, blow ye winds over the ocean
And bring back my bonny to me
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Last night as I lay on my pillow
Last night as I lay on my bed
Last night as I lay on my pillow
I dreamed my poor bonnie was dead
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
The winds have blown over the ocean
The winds have blown over the sea
The winds have blown over the ocean
and brought back my bonnie to me
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
Brought back, brought back
oh brought back my bonny to me to me
Brought back, brought back
oh brought back my bonny to me
{end_of_chorus}

{comment: Andere versie}

{start_of_verse}
My granny went down to the cellar
to see where the gassleak might be
She stroke up a match to see hither
oh bring back my granny to me
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse}
My Bonnie has tuberculosis.
My Bonnie has only one lung.
My Bonnie can cough up raw oysters.
And roll them around on her tongue, her tongue.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse}
My Bonnie leaned over the gastank.
The height of its contents to see.
I lighted a match to assist her.
Oh bring back my Bonnie to me, to me.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse}
Last night as I lay on my pillow.
Last night as I lay on my bed.
I stuck my feet out of the window.
Next morning the neighbours were dead, were dead.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse}
My breakfast lies over the ocean.
My luncheon lies over the rail.
My dinner is still in commotion.
Won't someone please bring me a pail, a pail.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse}
Who knows what I had for breakfast.
Who knows what I had for tea.
Who knows what I had for supper.
Just look out of the window and see, and see.
{end_of_verse}
